"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.5 and positive surgical margins. The pathological findings show an ISUP grade group 2 (Gleason 3+4) with pT2c disease, but the absence of nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Seminal vesicle invasion was absent, which is favorable.\n\nThe primary limitation impacting prognosis assessment is the lack of postoperative PSA data and follow-up information. The biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing, preventing comparison to the prostatectomy specimen findings. Given the positive margins and elevated preoperative PSA, these factors suggest a higher risk profile despite no documented seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. Future trajectory assessment critically depends on obtaining postoperative PSA levels and monitoring their subsequent changes.\n\nThe fixed horizon is 62.79 months with an expected event of 0. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
